That fits the Caterpillar’s exchange in **Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar.”** His question, “So you think you’re changed, do you?”, does not reassure Alice or tell her who she is; it makes her defend an unstable description of herself. His follow-up questions turn her confusion into something she has to examine rather than simply escape. The connection is not exact—the Caterpillar is more cryptic than a philosophical guide—but the scene invites a Socratic reading: confusion may be the beginning of questioning what we thought we knew.

